cards('05', r'''
4|The normal equations express least squares as X-transpose X times weights equals X-transpose y. Explicitly inverting the Gram matrix is less stable than a direct least-squares factorization and fails under rank deficiency.|X=np.array([[1.,0.],[1.,1.],[1.,2.]]); y=np.array([1.,3.,5.]); weights=np.linalg.solve(X.T@X,X.T@y); assert np.allclose(weights,[1,2]); print(weights)
5|Batch gradient descent uses all training observations to compute one average gradient. For a no-intercept line, the MSE gradient is twice the average of x times prediction minus target.|x=np.array([1.,2.,3.]); y=2*x; w=0.; gradient=2*np.mean(x*(w*x-y)); new_w=w-.05*gradient; assert np.mean((new_w*x-y)**2)<np.mean((w*x-y)**2); print(gradient,new_w)
6|Stochastic gradient descent updates using one observation at a time. Its gradient estimate is noisy, and observation order and step size affect the optimization path.|x=np.array([1.,2.,3.]); y=2*x; w=0.; history=[]
7|Mini-batch descent uses a small group of observations for each update, trading gradient noise against computation. Shuffling belongs only within the training set.|x=np.array([1.,2.,3.,4.]); y=2*x; w=0.; gradient=2*np.mean(x[:2]*(w*x[:2]-y[:2])); w-=.05*gradient; assert w==.5; print('First mini-batch weight:',w)
8|Polynomial regression constructs powers of inputs and fits a linear model in those expanded features. It is linear in its coefficients, even though its predictions curve as a function of the original input.|from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures; from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression; from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline; x=np.arange(6.).reshape(-1,1); y=x[:,0]**2; model=make_pipeline(PolynomialFeatures(2,include_bias=False),LinearRegression()).fit(x,y); assert np.allclose(model.predict([[2.5]]),[6.25]); print(model.predict([[2.5]]))
22|Regression metrics emphasize different errors. MAE has target units, MSE has squared target units, and R² compares squared error to a constant test-mean reference; R² can be negative.|from sklearn.metrics import r2_score; truth=[1,2,3]; prediction=[0,0,0]; score=r2_score(truth,prediction); assert score<0; print('Negative R²:',score)
23|Residuals are observed minus predicted values. Systematic residual patterns can reveal missing structure or nonconstant error spread; a zero mean residual alone is not sufficient evidence of adequacy.|x=np.arange(5.); truth=x*x; prediction=4*x-2; residual=truth-prediction; assert residual.tolist()==[2,-1,-2,-1,2]; print('Curved residual pattern:',residual)
24|Multicollinearity means predictors contain exact or near linear redundancy. Predictions may remain useful while individual coefficients become unstable or nonidentifiable.|X=np.array([[1.,2.],[2.,4.],[3.,6.]]); assert np.linalg.matrix_rank(X)==1; assert np.allclose(X@[3,0],X@[1,1]); print('Rank:',np.linalg.matrix_rank(X))
25|Linear-model inference assumptions are distinct from simply fitting predictions. Independence, error structure, and model specification influence standard errors and tests; normality of input features is not a general requirement for fitting OLS.|x=np.array([0.,1.,2.]); errors=np.array([1.,1.,1.]); print('Perfectly correlated illustrative errors:',errors); assert np.std(errors)==0
''')
