cards('07', r'''
3|Entropy and Gini measure label mixture within a node. A balanced binary node has entropy log 2 nats and Gini .5; a pure node has both zero.|p=np.array([.5,.5]); entropy=-np.sum(p*np.log(p)); gini=1-np.sum(p**2); assert np.isclose(entropy,np.log(2)) and gini==.5; print(entropy,gini)
4|Information gain subtracts the child-size-weighted impurity from parent impurity. Weighting matters: splitting a tiny pure child does not purify the entire dataset.|parent=.5; sizes=np.array([1,3]); impurities=np.array([0.,4/9]); child=np.average(impurities,weights=sizes); gain=parent-child; assert np.isclose(gain,1/6); print('Gini reduction:',gain)
12|XGBoost is an external optimized boosting library with its own regularized objectives and APIs. The included sklearn histogram model is an explicit fallback, not an XGBoost implementation. No external package is installed automatically.|from sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingClassifier; fallback=HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=20,random_state=42); print('Fallback only:',type(fallback).__name__)
13|LightGBM is an external histogram boosting library with leaf-wise growth and configuration different from sklearn estimators. The local fallback demonstrates boosting broadly and must not be reported as a LightGBM experiment.|from sklearn.ensemble import HistGradientBoostingClassifier; fallback=HistGradientBoostingClassifier(max_iter=20,max_leaf_nodes=7,random_state=42); print('Fallback only:',type(fallback).__name__)
14|CatBoost is an external boosting library with mechanisms for categorical features and ordered training. One-hot encoding plus sklearn boosting is a fallback, not an equivalent implementation of those mechanisms.|from sklearn.preprocessing import OneHotEncoder; encoder=OneHotEncoder(sparse_output=False).fit([['A'],['B']]); assert encoder.transform([['A']]).shape==(1,2); print('Fallback categorical representation:',encoder.transform([['A']]))
15|Hard voting counts class decisions; soft voting averages aligned class probabilities. Probability columns must correspond to the same labels, and probability calibration affects interpretation.|probabilities=np.array([[.8,.2],[.3,.7],[.6,.4]]); averaged=probabilities.mean(axis=0); assert averaged.argmax()==0; print('Soft-vote probabilities:',averaged)
16|Stacking trains a final estimator on base-model predictions generated without fitting the base models on those same validation rows. In-sample base predictions would leak training information into the meta-model.|fold_ids=np.array([0,0,1,1]); train_ids=np.flatnonzero(fold_ids!=0); validation_ids=np.flatnonzero(fold_ids==0); assert not(set(train_ids)&set(validation_ids)); print('One out-of-fold boundary:',train_ids,validation_ids)
17|Blending trains a meta-model on a reserved validation subset instead of cross-validated out-of-fold predictions. It is simpler but uses fewer observations for base fitting and meta fitting.|base_training=np.arange(6); blend_validation=np.arange(6,8); final_test=np.arange(8,10); assert not(set(blend_validation)&set(final_test)); print(base_training,blend_validation,final_test)
18|Bagging builds varied models largely independently and averages them; boosting adds models sequentially to correct an objective. Parallelism, noise sensitivity, and tuning behavior differ.|base=np.array([2.,4.,6.]); bagged=base.mean(); stages=np.array([1.,.5,.25]); boosted=stages.sum(); assert bagged==4 and boosted==1.75; print('Illustrative aggregation:',bagged,boosted)
''')

cards('08', r'''
1|A holdout set estimates performance on data excluded from fitting. A single random split can be variable, and grouped or chronological data require a different boundary.|from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split; train,test=train_test_split(np.arange(20),test_size=.25,random_state=42); assert len(test)==5 and not(set(train)&set(test)); print(train,test)
2|K-fold cross-validation rotates a validation fold while fitting on the remainder. Aggregate scores across folds, but do not pretend folds are fully independent experiments.|from sklearn.model_selection import KFold; folds=list(KFold(3,shuffle=True,random_state=42).split(np.arange(9))); assert sorted(np.concatenate([v for t,v in folds]).tolist())==list(range(9)); print(folds)
3|Stratified folds approximately preserve class frequencies, reducing missing-class folds in classification. Stratification does not account for repeated entities or future chronology.|from sklearn.model_selection import StratifiedKFold; labels=np.array([0]*6+[1]*6); folds=list(StratifiedKFold(3).split(np.zeros((12,1)),labels)); assert all(labels[v].sum()==2 for t,v in folds); print(folds)
4|Grouped validation keeps all observations from an entity in the same fold. Otherwise a model can recognize an entity rather than generalize to new entities.|from sklearn.model_selection import GroupKFold; groups=np.array([1,1,2,2,3,3]); folds=list(GroupKFold(3).split(np.zeros((6,1)),groups=groups)); assert all(not(set(groups[t])&set(groups[v])) for t,v in folds); print(folds)
5|Time-series validation trains on earlier observations and validates on later ones. Random shuffling can let future information influence a historical forecast.|from sklearn.model_selection import TimeSeriesSplit; folds=list(TimeSeriesSplit(3).split(np.arange(12))); assert all(t.max()<v.min() for t,v in folds); print(folds)
6|Cross-validation leakage occurs when preprocessing or feature selection uses all rows before folds are created. Wrap fitted transformations inside a Pipeline so each training fold supplies its own statistics.|from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline; from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler; from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge; pipeline=make_pipeline(StandardScaler(),Ridge()); assert len(pipeline.steps)==2; print(pipeline)
7|GridSearchCV evaluates a specified Cartesian grid with internal validation. More candidates increase both compute and the risk of overfitting the validation procedure; reserve a final test boundary.|from sklearn.model_selection import GridSearchCV; from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge; search=GridSearchCV(Ridge(),{'alpha':[.1,1.,10.]},cv=3); assert len(search.param_grid['alpha'])==3; print(search)
8|RandomizedSearchCV samples configurations from a defined distribution. Specify the random seed and distributions; uniform sampling of a large scale parameter may waste trials compared with logarithmic sampling.|from sklearn.model_selection import ParameterSampler; candidates=list(ParameterSampler({'alpha':np.logspace(-3,3,20)},n_iter=4,random_state=42)); assert len(candidates)==4; print(candidates)
9|Bayesian optimization uses a surrogate model and an acquisition rule to propose promising next trials. The toy expected-improvement calculation illustrates only the acquisition mechanism, not a complete optimizer.|from scipy.stats import norm; incumbent=.5; mean=.4; std=.1; improvement=incumbent-mean; z=improvement/std; ei=improvement*norm.cdf(z)+std*norm.pdf(z); assert ei>0; print('Expected improvement for minimization:',ei)
10|Nested validation separates model selection in inner folds from evaluation in outer folds. It estimates the selection procedure rather than only one optimistically chosen configuration.|outer_train=np.arange(8); outer_test=np.arange(8,10); inner_train=outer_train[:6]; inner_validation=outer_train[6:]; assert not(set(inner_validation)&set(outer_test)); print(inner_train,inner_validation,outer_test)
11|Learning curves vary training size while maintaining a validation protocol. They can suggest data limitations or capacity issues but do not prescribe one universal remedy.|sizes=np.array([10,50,100]); train=np.array([.99,.94,.91]); validation=np.array([.6,.8,.87]); assert sizes.shape==validation.shape; print('Illustrative gaps:',train-validation)
12|Validation curves vary a model setting with training size held conceptually fixed. Use cross-validation and keep the final test untouched when choosing the setting.|complexity=np.array([1,3,10]); validation=np.array([.7,.85,.72]); chosen=complexity[validation.argmax()]; assert chosen==3; print('Illustrative selected complexity:',chosen)
13|Feature selection is part of model fitting when it uses target information. Recompute selections inside each training fold instead of selecting globally before validation.|from sklearn.feature_selection import SelectKBest,f_regression; selector=SelectKBest(f_regression,k=2); assert selector.k==2; print(selector)
14|Recursive Feature Elimination repeatedly fits an estimator and removes lower-ranked features. Correlated features can make the selected subset unstable, and the full procedure belongs inside validation.|from sklearn.feature_selection import RFE; from sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression; selector=RFE(LinearRegression(),n_features_to_select=2,step=1); assert selector.n_features_to_select==2; print(selector)
15|Threshold optimization should reflect costs or constraints on validation data. It is distinct from fitting the probability model and must not optimize against final test labels.|cost_fp=1.; cost_fn=4.; calibrated_threshold=cost_fp/(cost_fp+cost_fn); assert calibrated_threshold==.2; print('Cost-derived threshold under calibrated-probability assumptions:',calibrated_threshold)
16|Class weights alter the fitting objective; resampling alters the training distribution. Apply resampling only inside training folds and evaluate on realistic outcome frequencies.|counts=np.array([90,10]); weights=counts.sum()/(2*counts); assert np.allclose(weights,[5/9,5]); print('Balanced class weights:',weights)
17|Error analysis examines where a model fails, including segments, missingness, and unusual cases. Searching many segments after observing errors can overfit the analysis itself.|truth=np.array([1,0,1,0]); prediction=np.array([1,1,0,0]); wrong=np.flatnonzero(truth!=prediction); assert wrong.tolist()==[1,2]; print('Error indices:',wrong)
18|Compare models on matched splits and metrics so differences are interpretable. A small score difference may be noise rather than a reliable improvement.|scores_a=np.array([.8,.82,.79]); scores_b=np.array([.81,.81,.8]); differences=scores_b-scores_a; assert differences.shape==(3,); print('Paired differences:',differences)
19|Evaluation scores are estimates with uncertainty from sampled observations and fitting choices. Bootstrap examples require assumptions about independence; repeated entities must be resampled appropriately.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); correctness=np.array([1,1,0,1,0,1]); bootstrap=np.array([rng.choice(correctness,size=6,replace=True).mean() for _ in range(200)]); print('Illustrative bootstrap percentiles:',np.quantile(bootstrap,[.025,.975])); assert bootstrap.shape==(200,)
''')

