for row in [
    (1,'KMeans','n_clusters=3, n_init=10, random_state=42','K-Means alternates nearest-center assignments and center means to reduce squared within-cluster distances. It favors compact groups and depends on feature scales and chosen cluster count.'),
    (2,'KMeans','n_clusters=3, init="k-means++", n_init=10, random_state=42','K-Means++ chooses initial centers with distance-squared weighting to spread them out. Initialization improves the starting state but does not guarantee a global optimum.'),
    (3,'MiniBatchKMeans','n_clusters=3, n_init=3, batch_size=20, random_state=42','Mini-Batch K-Means approximates center updates using small groups of observations. It saves compute with a different optimization path and potentially worse reconstruction objective.'),
    (4,'AgglomerativeClustering','n_clusters=3, linkage="complete"','Hierarchical clustering builds a sequence of merges or splits. Linkage defines distance between groups; a hierarchy is a representation, not an automatically correct taxonomy.'),
    (5,'AgglomerativeClustering','n_clusters=3, linkage="ward"','Agglomerative clustering repeatedly merges groups. Ward linkage minimizes increases in within-cluster squared dispersion and assumes Euclidean geometry.'),
    (6,'DBSCAN','eps=0.8, min_samples=4','DBSCAN expands connected dense neighborhoods and marks other points as noise. Epsilon has distance-scale meaning; clusters need not be spherical, but varying density can cause failure.'),
    (7,'OPTICS','min_samples=4, xi=0.05, min_cluster_size=0.1','OPTICS records a density-based reachability ordering over neighborhood scales. Extracting labels from that ordering is a separate choice and still depends on representation.'),
    (8,'HDBSCAN','min_cluster_size=5','HDBSCAN studies a hierarchy of density connectivity and extracts stable groups. Stability in the algorithmic hierarchy is not proof of semantic usefulness.'),
    (11,'SpectralClustering','n_clusters=3, affinity="nearest_neighbors", n_neighbors=8, random_state=42','Spectral clustering partitions a similarity graph using spectral coordinates. Graph construction and connectivity matter; it is more expensive than simple centroid assignment.'),
    (12,'MeanShift','bandwidth=1.0, bin_seeding=True','Mean Shift moves points toward local density peaks within a bandwidth. Bandwidth controls resolution and can produce too many or too few groups.'),
    (13,'AffinityPropagation','damping=0.8, random_state=42','Affinity Propagation exchanges responsibility and availability messages to select exemplars. Preference affects cluster count and convergence is not guaranteed.'),
]: cluster(*row)
cards('09', r'''
9|A Gaussian mixture represents a distribution as weighted Gaussian components. Responsibilities are soft memberships, not known category labels; covariance structure controls component shape.|from sklearn.mixture import GaussianMixture; X=np.array([[-2.],[-1.8],[-2.2],[2.],[1.8],[2.2]]); model=GaussianMixture(2,random_state=42).fit(X); membership=model.predict_proba(X); assert np.allclose(membership.sum(axis=1),1); print(membership)
10|Expectation-Maximization alternates expected latent assignments and parameter updates. For mixtures it generally finds a local likelihood solution and can need several initializations.|likelihood=np.array([[.8,.2],[.3,.7]]); weights=np.array([.5,.5]); joint=likelihood*weights; responsibilities=joint/joint.sum(axis=1,keepdims=True); updated=responsibilities.mean(axis=0); assert np.allclose(updated,[.55,.45]); print(responsibilities,updated)
14|Silhouette compares mean within-cluster distance with distance to the nearest other cluster. It requires a valid nontrivial partition and can prefer compact separated geometry over useful domain categories.|from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score; X=np.array([[0.],[.1],[5.],[5.1]]); labels=np.array([0,0,1,1]); score=silhouette_score(X,labels); assert score>.9; print(score)
15|Cluster plots are diagnostic projections; overlapping points in two dimensions may be separated elsewhere. Choose algorithms using geometry, scale, noise policy, and the intended use of groups.|choices={'compact groups':'K-Means','dense irregular groups':'DBSCAN','soft distributions':'Gaussian mixture'}; assert len(choices)==3; print(choices)
''')

REDUCERS = {}
def reducer(number,module,name,args,note):
    ident=f'10-{number:02d}'
    REDUCERS[ident]=dict(module=module,name=name,arguments=args)
    CARDS[ident]=dict(explanation=note,example='')
for row in [
    (1,'decomposition','PCA','n_components=2','PCA centers observations and chooses orthogonal directions of maximal variance. High variance is not automatically predictive relevance, and scales determine what dominates.'),
    (4,'decomposition','KernelPCA','n_components=2, kernel="rbf", gamma=0.5','Kernel PCA performs variance-oriented analysis in an implicit nonlinear feature space. Kernel settings change the geometry and out-of-sample transformations depend on the fitted reference set.'),
    (5,'decomposition','FastICA','n_components=2, max_iter=1000, random_state=42','ICA seeks components with statistical independence under assumptions including non-Gaussian structure. Components have sign and order ambiguities; independence is stronger than zero correlation.'),
    (6,'decomposition','FactorAnalysis','n_components=2, random_state=42','Factor Analysis models observations as low-dimensional latent factors plus feature-specific noise. The statistical noise model distinguishes it from deterministic PCA reconstruction.'),
    (7,'decomposition','NMF','n_components=2, init="nndsvda", max_iter=1000, random_state=42','NMF approximates a nonnegative table using nonnegative factors. It encourages additive parts but solutions can be nonunique and initialization sensitive.'),
    (8,'manifold','TSNE','n_components=2, perplexity=10, max_iter=300, init="pca", learning_rate="auto", random_state=42','t-SNE emphasizes local similarities in a low-dimensional visualization. Cluster distances, areas, and apparent gaps in the plot should not be read as faithful global geometry.'),
    (10,'random_projection','GaussianRandomProjection','n_components=2, random_state=42','Random projection maps inputs through a seeded random matrix to approximately preserve distances under appropriate dimension bounds. Two output dimensions are a visualization toy, not a general guarantee.'),
]: reducer(*row)
cards('10', r'''
2|Scratch PCA centers each feature using training means, decomposes the centered table, and projects onto selected right singular vectors. This essential mechanism does not implement every library solver or whitening option.|X=np.array([[1.,2.],[2.,4.],[3.,6.]]); centered=X-X.mean(axis=0); U,s,Vt=np.linalg.svd(centered,full_matrices=False); scores=centered@Vt[:1].T; assert np.linalg.matrix_rank(centered)==1; print(scores)
3|SVD separates an observation-by-feature table into orthonormal directions and singular strengths. Unlike PCA, the decomposition itself does not necessarily center the table.|A=np.array([[3.,0.],[0.,1.],[0.,0.]]); U,s,Vt=np.linalg.svd(A,full_matrices=False); assert np.allclose((U*s)@Vt,A); print(s)
9|UMAP is an optional manifold-learning package. Its graph construction and embedding choices affect local and global structure; a PCA fallback is a different algorithm and must be labeled accordingly.|from sklearn.decomposition import PCA; fallback=PCA(n_components=2); print('Explicit fallback, not UMAP:',type(fallback).__name__)
11|Feature selection keeps named original columns, whereas extraction creates combinations or latent coordinates. Original-column interpretation is easier, but selected columns need not preserve all useful structure.|X=np.array([[1.,2.,3.],[4.,5.,6.]]); selected=X[:,[0,2]]; extracted=X@np.array([[1.],[1.],[1.]]); assert selected.shape==(2,2) and extracted.shape==(2,1); print(selected,extracted)
12|An autoencoder learns an encoder and decoder to reconstruct observations through a bottleneck. A linear bottleneck relates to low-rank reconstruction; nonlinear networks have a different optimization problem.|X=np.array([[1.,2.],[2.,4.],[3.,6.]]); mean=X.mean(axis=0); centered=X-mean; U,s,Vt=np.linalg.svd(centered,full_matrices=False); encoded=centered@Vt[:1].T; decoded=encoded@Vt[:1]+mean; assert np.allclose(decoded,X); print('Linear bottleneck reconstruction:',decoded)
''')

cards('11', r'''
1|Statistical anomaly detection compares observations with a reference distribution. Unusual does not mean fraudulent, and a threshold needs a cost and false-alert policy.|reference=np.array([9.,10.,11.]); candidate=20.; z=(candidate-reference.mean())/reference.std(); assert z>3; print('Reference-standardized anomaly score:',z)
2|Z-scores use means and standard deviations; robust alternatives use medians and absolute deviations. A zero scale requires a separate policy rather than division by zero.|reference=np.array([1.,2.,2.,3.,100.]); median=np.median(reference); mad=np.median(abs(reference-median)); score=.6745*(reference-median)/mad; assert score[-1]>10; print(score)
3|Isolation Forest scores points by how quickly randomized splits isolate them. Easy isolation is a geometric anomaly signal, not a verified fraud label.|from sklearn.ensemble import IsolationForest; X=np.array([[0.],[.1],[-.1],[.2],[8.]]); model=IsolationForest(n_estimators=30,contamination=.2,random_state=42).fit(X); print('Labels and anomaly-oriented scores:',model.predict(X),-model.score_samples(X))
4|Local Outlier Factor compares local density with neighboring densities. In novelty mode fit on a reference set and score new points; the default training-set outlier mode has a different API.|from sklearn.neighbors import LocalOutlierFactor; reference=np.linspace(-1,1,20).reshape(-1,1); model=LocalOutlierFactor(n_neighbors=5,novelty=True).fit(reference); print('Novelty labels:',model.predict([[0.],[8.]]))
5|One-Class SVM learns a boundary around a reference distribution using a kernel. Nu and kernel scale govern the boundary and rejection behavior; the reference set must represent normal operation.|from sklearn.svm import OneClassSVM; X=np.linspace(-1,1,20).reshape(-1,1); model=OneClassSVM(nu=.1,gamma=.5).fit(X); print(model.predict([[0.],[8.]]))
6|Robust covariance models a central ellipsoidal distribution while reducing some outlier influence. Its assumptions can fail for multimodal or very high-dimensional reference data.|from sklearn.covariance import EllipticEnvelope; rng=np.random.default_rng(42); reference=rng.normal(size=(60,2)); model=EllipticEnvelope(contamination=.1,random_state=42).fit(reference); print(model.predict([[0.,0.],[8.,8.]]))
7|Autoencoder anomaly detection uses reconstruction discrepancy as a score. Train on an appropriate normal reference and validate thresholds separately; a flexible autoencoder can reconstruct anomalies too.|normal=np.array([[0.,0.],[1.,1.],[2.,2.]]); mean=normal.mean(axis=0); direction=np.array([1.,1.])/np.sqrt(2); point=np.array([2.,-2.]); reconstructed=((point-mean)@direction)*direction+mean; error=np.sum((point-reconstructed)**2); assert error==8; print('Linear-bottleneck score:',error)
8|Evaluate anomaly scores with labels and an operating threshold when available. Precision-recall metrics and alert volume can be more useful than accuracy on a rare-event problem.|from sklearn.metrics import average_precision_score; truth=[0,0,0,1]; anomaly_scores=[.1,.2,.3,.9]; assert average_precision_score(truth,anomaly_scores)==1; print('Perfect ranking on four synthetic points, not a population claim.')
9|Suspicious transactions require temporal availability, investigation capacity, and delayed-label handling. Large amounts alone are not proof of fraud, and customer repetition changes validation design.|amount=np.array([10.,20.,1000.]); reference_limit=100.; alerts=amount>reference_limit; assert alerts.tolist()==[False,False,True]; print('Review candidates, not fraud conclusions:',alerts)
10|Sensor anomaly detection compares current readings with a reference appropriate to season and operating state. A legitimate mode change can resemble an anomaly under a stale reference.|reference=np.array([99.,100.,101.]); current=np.array([100.,130.]); score=abs(current-reference.mean()); assert score.tolist()==[0,30]; print('Deviation magnitudes:',score)
''')

cards('12', r'''
1|Time series have ordered observations. Preserve timestamp order and distinguish forecasting future values from explaining values already observed.|dates=pd.date_range('2024-01-01',periods=5,freq='D'); series=pd.Series([10,11,12,13,14],index=dates); assert series.index.is_monotonic_increasing; print(series)
2|Trend describes persistent level movement, seasonality recurring patterns, and stationarity a stability condition on distributional behavior. Detrending a series does not prove stationarity.|t=np.arange(12); trend=.5*t; seasonal=2*np.sin(2*np.pi*t/4); series=10+trend+seasonal; assert np.allclose(series-seasonal,10+trend); print(series)
3|A causal moving average uses only values available at that time. A centered rolling window includes future observations and is unsuitable as a historical forecast feature.|series=pd.Series([1.,2.,3.,4.,5.]); rolling=series.shift(1).rolling(3).mean(); assert rolling.iloc[3]==2; print('Prior-only rolling means:',rolling)
4|Simple exponential smoothing updates a level as alpha times the latest observation plus one-minus-alpha times the old level. It is different from an exponential trend model.|level=10.; observation=14.; alpha=.25; updated=alpha*observation+(1-alpha)*level; assert updated==11; print('Updated level:',updated)
5|An autoregression predicts using lagged observations. The number of lags controls information and complexity; stability and residual assumptions need separate checks.|history=np.array([8.,10.]); intercept=1.; coefficient=.8; next_prediction=intercept+coefficient*history[-1]; assert next_prediction==9; print(next_prediction)
6|A time-series moving-average model uses past innovation errors, not a rolling average of observed values. Those errors are latent and normally estimated jointly with parameters.|mean=10.; theta=.5; previous_innovation=2.; predicted=mean+theta*previous_innovation; assert predicted==11; print('MA(1) conditional mean:',predicted)
7|ARMA combines lagged observations and lagged innovations. It generally targets stationary behavior; specifying orders alone does not establish a valid fitted model.|mean=1.; phi=.7; last_value=4.; theta=.2; last_error=-1.; prediction=mean+phi*last_value+theta*last_error; assert np.isclose(prediction,3.6); print(prediction)
8|ARIMA adds differencing to an ARMA model. Differencing transforms levels into changes; reconstruct a level forecast by accumulating predicted changes from the last known level.|levels=np.array([10.,12.,15.]); differences=np.diff(levels); next_change=2.; next_level=levels[-1]+next_change; assert differences.tolist()==[2,3] and next_level==17; print(differences,next_level)
9|SARIMA includes seasonal autoregressive, differencing, and moving-average terms. Seasonal differencing subtracts the observation one period earlier; it should match a justified seasonal period.|values=np.array([10.,12.,14.,11.,13.,15.]); period=3; seasonal_difference=values[period:]-values[:-period]; assert seasonal_difference.tolist()==[1,1,1]; print(seasonal_difference)
10|Feature-based forecasting constructs supervised rows from information available before a forecast origin. Features such as known calendar variables differ from future demand that has not yet occurred.|series=pd.Series([10.,12.,11.,14.,13.]); frame=pd.DataFrame({'lag1':series.shift(1),'target':series}).dropna(); assert frame.iloc[0].tolist()==[10,12]; print(frame)
11|Shift before rolling when predicting the current target, so its value cannot enter its own feature. Also account for when raw observations become available operationally.|series=pd.Series([1.,2.,3.,4.]); feature=series.shift(1).rolling(2).mean(); assert feature.iloc[2]==1.5; print(feature)
12|Time-series cross-validation expands or rolls a historical training window and evaluates later observations. Use gaps when label horizons or feature construction overlap boundaries.|from sklearn.model_selection import TimeSeriesSplit; splits=list(TimeSeriesSplit(3,gap=1).split(np.arange(20))); assert all(v.min()-t.max()>1 for t,v in splits); print(splits)
13|A Random Forest can forecast from lag and calendar features without directly modeling temporal dependence. Trees do not extrapolate a trend beyond their trained target range.|from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestRegressor; X=np.arange(20.).reshape(-1,1); y=np.sin(X[:,0]/3); model=RandomForestRegressor(n_estimators=20,random_state=42).fit(X[:15],y[:15]); print('Chronological holdout predictions:',model.predict(X[15:]))
14|Gradient boosting forecasts from causal features through additive trees. Preserve chronology during tuning, and compare against a last-value or seasonal baseline.|from sklearn.ensemble import GradientBoostingRegressor; X=np.arange(20.).reshape(-1,1); y=np.sin(X[:,0]/3); model=GradientBoostingRegressor(n_estimators=20,random_state=42).fit(X[:15],y[:15]); print(model.predict(X[15:]))
15|An LSTM keeps a gated cell state and hidden state to process sequences. A gating toy does not constitute a trained forecasting network; sequence windows and chronological evaluation still need design.|previous_cell=2.; forget_gate=.8; input_gate=.3; candidate=.5; cell=forget_gate*previous_cell+input_gate*candidate; assert np.isclose(cell,1.75); print('Illustrative cell-state update:',cell)
16|MAE and RMSE preserve forecast target units. Percentage errors can be undefined near zero; compare to a naive baseline with the same forecast horizon.|truth=np.array([10.,0.,20.]); forecast=np.array([12.,1.,18.]); mae=np.abs(truth-forecast).mean(); rmse=np.sqrt(np.mean((truth-forecast)**2)); assert np.isclose(mae,5/3); print(mae,rmse)
17|Future leakage can arise from centered windows, random splitting, revised data, or fitting transformations globally. Forecast features must reflect what was actually known at the origin.|known_until=3; required_indices=np.array([1,2,3]); forbidden_index=4; assert required_indices.max()<=known_until and forbidden_index>known_until; print('Historical availability boundary:',known_until)
18|Advanced forecasting libraries provide additional model families and training tooling. They remain optional; no package or model download is performed here. A local naive forecast supplies a transparent comparison.|history=np.array([4.,5.,6.]); horizon=3; naive=np.repeat(history[-1],horizon); assert naive.tolist()==[6,6,6]; print('Last-value baseline:',naive)
''')

cards('13', r'''
1|Bayesian inference combines a prior distribution and likelihood into a normalized posterior. The posterior changes with evidence and assumptions; it is not merely a point estimate with a confidence interval.|prior=np.array([.6,.4]); likelihood=np.array([.2,.8]); joint=prior*likelihood; posterior=joint/joint.sum(); assert np.allclose(posterior,[3/11,8/11]); print(posterior)
2|Prior describes beliefs before the current observations, likelihood scores observations given parameters, and posterior conditions on observed evidence. A likelihood need not sum to one over parameter candidates.|prior=np.array([.5,.5]); likelihood=np.array([.1,.9]); posterior=prior*likelihood; posterior/=posterior.sum(); assert np.allclose(posterior,[.1,.9]); print(posterior)
3|Maximum likelihood maximizes the observation probability under a chosen model without a prior penalty. For independent coin flips, the MLE is the observed success fraction.|observations=np.array([1,1,0,1,0]); estimate=observations.mean(); assert estimate==.6; print('Coin probability MLE:',estimate)
4|MAP maximizes posterior density and therefore includes a prior. For a Beta prior and Bernoulli observations, the interior mode differs from the posterior mean and from the MLE.|successes=3; failures=1; alpha=2; beta=2; map_estimate=(successes+alpha-1)/(successes+failures+alpha+beta-2); posterior_mean=(successes+alpha)/(successes+failures+alpha+beta); assert np.isclose(map_estimate,2/3) and posterior_mean==.625; print(map_estimate,posterior_mean)
5|Bayesian linear regression combines a prior on weights with a likelihood for outcomes. In a Gaussian scalar example, precisions add; posterior uncertainty remains conditional on the specified noise and prior.|prior_mean=0.; prior_variance=1.; observed=2.; noise_variance=1.; posterior_variance=1/(1/prior_variance+1/noise_variance); posterior_mean=posterior_variance*(prior_mean/prior_variance+observed/noise_variance); assert posterior_mean==1 and posterior_variance==.5; print(posterior_mean,posterior_variance)
6|A Gaussian mixture combines component densities using nonnegative weights summing to one. Soft responsibilities quantify model membership, not verified group identities.|from sklearn.mixture import GaussianMixture; X=np.array([[-2.],[-1.],[1.],[2.]]); model=GaussianMixture(2,random_state=42).fit(X); probabilities=model.predict_proba(X); assert np.allclose(probabilities.sum(axis=1),1); print(probabilities)
7|A Hidden Markov Model combines hidden-state transitions with observation likelihoods. The forward recursion marginalizes state paths; it does not reveal a known true hidden sequence.|transition=np.array([[.8,.2],[.3,.7]]); initial=np.array([.6,.4]); emission=np.array([.9,.2]); first=initial*emission; next_forward=(first@transition)*emission; likelihood=next_forward.sum(); assert np.isclose(likelihood,.4432); print('Two-observation likelihood:',likelihood)
8|A Gaussian Process specifies distributions over functions through a mean and covariance kernel. Predictive uncertainty depends on kernel and noise assumptions and can be unreliable under model misspecification.|from sklearn.gaussian_process import GaussianProcessRegressor; from sklearn.gaussian_process.kernels import RBF; X=np.array([[0.],[1.],[2.]]); y=np.array([0.,1.,0.]); model=GaussianProcessRegressor(kernel=RBF(),alpha=.01,optimizer=None).fit(X,y); mean,std=model.predict([[1.5]],return_std=True); assert std[0]>=0; print(mean,std)
9|Variational inference chooses an approximate posterior family and optimizes a lower bound. Restricted families can miss multimodality; computational convenience does not imply exact Bayesian inference.|log_evidence=-2.; kl_gap=.3; elbo=log_evidence-kl_gap; assert elbo<=log_evidence; print('Illustrative evidence lower bound:',elbo)
10|Monte Carlo estimates expectations by averaging simulated draws. Its random error decreases with sample count under suitable conditions, but dependent chains need additional diagnostics.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); draws=rng.normal(size=10000); estimate=np.mean(draws**2); assert np.isfinite(estimate); print('Estimate of standard-normal second moment:',estimate)
11|Uncertainty includes outcome noise and uncertainty about fitted structure. Repeating fits can expose sensitivity, but a spread of predictions is not automatically a calibrated interval.|predictions=np.array([9.,10.,12.,11.]); mean=predictions.mean(); spread=predictions.std(); assert mean==10.5; print('Ensemble mean and descriptive spread:',mean,spread)
12|A probabilistic prediction specifies an outcome distribution, not only a central estimate. Evaluate sharpness together with calibration using proper scoring rules and held-out observations.|from scipy.stats import norm; observed=1.; log_score=-norm.logpdf(observed,loc=0,scale=1); assert np.isclose(log_score,1.4189385332); print('Negative log predictive density:',log_score)
''')

cards('14', r'''
1|An artificial neuron computes a weighted sum plus a bias and applies an activation. This is a mathematical abstraction, not a detailed model of a biological neuron.|inputs=np.array([2.,1.]); weights=np.array([.5,-1.]); bias=.2; score=inputs@weights+bias; assert np.isclose(score,.2); print('Neuron score:',score)
2|A perceptron converts a linear score to a class decision and updates on mistakes. Its convergence guarantee applies to separable data under specified conditions, not arbitrary datasets.|x=np.array([1.,2.]); w=np.zeros(2); label=1; prediction=0; updated=w+(label-prediction)*x; assert np.allclose(updated,[1,2]); print(updated)
3|Activations introduce nonlinearity. ReLU keeps positive scores and clips negative scores to zero; sigmoid bounds outputs but can saturate. Activation choice changes gradients and representation.|scores=np.array([-2.,0.,3.]); relu=np.maximum(scores,0); assert relu.tolist()==[0,0,3]; print(relu)
4|A feedforward network applies successive affine transformations and activations without a recurrent state. Matrix shapes must match the feature and layer dimensions.|X=np.array([[1.,2.]]); W1=np.array([[1.,-1.],[.5,.5]]); hidden=np.maximum(X@W1,0); W2=np.array([[1.],[2.]]); output=hidden@W2; assert output.shape==(1,1); print(hidden,output)
5|Network losses connect outputs to task-specific penalties. Regression squared error and classification cross-entropy are not interchangeable labels for the same objective.|predicted=np.array([2.,4.]); target=np.array([1.,5.]); mse=np.mean((predicted-target)**2); assert mse==1; print('Mean squared loss:',mse)
6|A computational graph records elementary operations and their dependencies. Backward derivatives multiply along paths and add when a variable affects the output through multiple paths.|x=3.; squared=x*x; output=squared+x; derivative=2*x+1; assert output==12 and derivative==7; print(output,derivative)
7|The chain rule propagates a scalar output derivative through an intermediate quantity. Keeping intermediate values avoids losing factors during manual differentiation.|x=2.; inner=x+1; output=inner**3; derivative=3*inner**2; assert output==27 and derivative==27; print(output,derivative)
8|Backpropagation by hand computes local derivatives then combines them backward. For a single linear prediction and squared loss, the weight gradient contains the input multiplier.|x=2.; weight=3.; target=5.; output=weight*x; residual=output-target; grad_output=2*residual; grad_weight=grad_output*x; assert grad_weight==4; print('Backward weight gradient:',grad_weight)
9|A NumPy network requires explicit forward values, derivatives, and parameter updates. The toy below differentiates one neuron only and is not a general multilayer training engine.|x=2.; target=1.; w=.5; bias=.2; score=w*x+bias; output=np.tanh(score); loss=(output-target)**2; gradient=2*(output-target)*(1-output**2)*x; assert np.isfinite(gradient); print(loss,gradient)
10|An MLP stacks fully connected layers with nonlinear activations. Hidden width and layer count are chosen hyperparameters; weights and biases are fitted. Scaling and bounded training budgets matter.|from sklearn.neural_network import MLPClassifier; model=MLPClassifier(hidden_layer_sizes=(8,),max_iter=100,random_state=42); assert model.hidden_layer_sizes==(8,); print('Small library MLP configuration:',model)
11|Initialization sets initial weights before training. Identical hidden units can remain symmetric; variance-scaled random initialization aims to keep signal magnitudes controlled across layers.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); fan_in=4; weights=rng.normal(0,np.sqrt(2/fan_in),size=(4,8)); assert weights.shape==(4,8); print('He-style target standard deviation:',np.sqrt(2/fan_in))
12|Repeated derivative multiplication can shrink or explode gradients. Activation derivatives, weight scales, normalization, and architecture affect this behavior; clipping only limits magnitude after computation.|shrinking=.5**20; growing=2.**20; assert shrinking<1e-5 and growing>1e6; print('Twenty-factor products:',shrinking,growing)
13|Stochastic optimization estimates gradients from subsets of training observations. It reduces per-step cost but makes the optimization path noisy; seed and batch order must be documented.|gradients=np.array([1.,-1.,3.,1.]); batch_gradient=gradients.mean(); sample_gradient=gradients[0]; assert batch_gradient==1; print(batch_gradient,sample_gradient)
14|SGD updates each parameter by subtracting a learning-rate times its gradient. A fixed step may overshoot; decay schedules change steps over time rather than changing the objective.|weight=2.; gradient=3.; rate=.1; updated=weight-rate*gradient; assert np.isclose(updated,1.7); print(updated)
15|Momentum accumulates a moving gradient direction and can accelerate consistent progress. Sign conventions differ across implementations; specify the recurrence before comparing values.|velocity=0.; beta=.9; gradient=2.; velocity=beta*velocity+gradient; weight=1.-.1*velocity; assert weight==.8; print(velocity,weight)
16|RMSprop tracks an exponential average of squared gradients and rescales updates. Epsilon prevents division by zero but is not an interchangeable learning rate.|average=0.; beta=.9; gradient=2.; average=beta*average+(1-beta)*gradient**2; update=.1*gradient/(np.sqrt(average)+1e-8); assert np.isclose(average,.4); print(average,update)
17|Adam combines first- and second-moment estimates with bias correction. These are optimization states, not Bayesian posterior moments. Weight decay may be coupled or decoupled depending on the optimizer.|gradient=2.; beta1=.9; beta2=.999; m=(1-beta1)*gradient; v=(1-beta2)*gradient**2; m_hat=m/(1-beta1); v_hat=v/(1-beta2); update=.1*m_hat/(np.sqrt(v_hat)+1e-8); assert np.isclose(update,.1); print(update)
18|Dropout randomly suppresses activations during training and uses a different inference behavior. Inverted dropout rescales retained activations so their expectation stays unchanged.|activation=np.array([2.,4.,6.]); keep_probability=.5; mask=np.array([1,0,1]); dropped=activation*mask/keep_probability; assert dropped.tolist()==[4,0,12]; print('One illustrative training mask:',dropped)
19|Batch normalization normalizes using batch statistics during training and stored running statistics during evaluation. Mixing modes can make predictions depend on the current request batch.|batch=np.array([1.,3.]); mean=batch.mean(); variance=batch.var(); normalized=(batch-mean)/np.sqrt(variance+1e-5); assert np.allclose(normalized,[-1,1],atol=1e-5); print(normalized)
20|Training computes gradients and updates parameters. Validation disables training-only behavior and measures held-out performance without updates. Separate those modes and report epoch metrics consistently.|training_ids=np.arange(6); validation_ids=np.arange(6,8); assert not(set(training_ids)&set(validation_ids)); print('Training and validation boundaries:',training_ids,validation_ids)
21|PyTorch tensors can track operations for automatic differentiation. Calling backward accumulates gradients; clear them deliberately between updates. PyTorch remains an optional installation for this draft.|import importlib.util; available=importlib.util.find_spec('torch') is not None; print('PyTorch installed:',available)
22|A PyTorch pipeline combines data loading, model modes, loss, backward computation, optimizer updates, and validation. CPU operation should be the default; no package is installed from this notebook.|training_order=['zero_grad','forward','loss','backward','optimizer.step']; assert training_order.index('backward')<training_order.index('optimizer.step'); print(training_order)
''')

cards('15', r'''
1|CNNs apply shared local filters across images. Weight sharing reduces parameter count compared with fully connected image layers, but padding and stride determine output size.|height=8; kernel=3; stride=1; padding=0; output=(height+2*padding-kernel)//stride+1; assert output==6; print('Output side length:',output)
2|Convolution combines a local patch and filter; pooling summarizes a region. Deep-learning libraries commonly implement cross-correlation without reversing the filter despite the conventional layer name.|patch=np.array([[1.,2.],[3.,4.]]); kernel=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,-1.]]); correlation=np.sum(patch*kernel); pooled=np.max(patch); assert correlation==-3 and pooled==4; print(correlation,pooled)
3|CNN image classification learns local features and aggregates them into label scores. Keep augmentation and preprocessing boundaries consistent; synthetic accuracy does not establish performance on photographs.|images=np.zeros((4,1,8,8)); labels=np.array([0,0,1,1]); images[2:,:,2:6,2:6]=1; assert images.shape==(4,1,8,8); print('Synthetic channel-first image batch:',images.shape,labels)
4|Transfer learning reuses a representation from a source task. Source data, preprocessing, and target domain influence its usefulness; transferring weights is not evidence that the domains match.|backbone_parameters=1000; head_parameters=30; trainable=head_parameters; assert trainable<backbone_parameters+head_parameters; print('Illustrative frozen-backbone trainable count:',trainable)
5|Fine-tuning updates some pretrained weights for a target task. This optional extension needs licensed weights and a controlled download plan; no model is downloaded here. Unfreezing can overfit small target datasets.|layers={'early':False,'late':True,'head':True}; assert sum(layers.values())==2; print('Illustrative trainable-layer policy:',layers)
6|An RNN updates a hidden state using current input and previous state. Reusing the same weights across timesteps differs from simply concatenating a whole sequence into one feature row.|hidden=.2; recurrent_weight=.5; input_weight=1.; value=.3; next_hidden=np.tanh(recurrent_weight*hidden+input_weight*value); assert np.isclose(next_hidden,np.tanh(.4)); print(next_hidden)
7|An LSTM uses gates to update a cell state separately from its hidden output. Gates can retain or replace information but do not guarantee learning arbitrarily long dependencies.|cell=2.; forget=.8; input_gate=.2; candidate=.5; updated=forget*cell+input_gate*candidate; assert np.isclose(updated,1.7); print(updated)
8|A GRU mixes previous and candidate hidden states with a gate. Recurrence conventions differ across libraries; state the exact formula before comparing gate interpretations.|old=1.; candidate=-1.; retention=.75; new=retention*old+(1-retention)*candidate; assert new==.5; print('Retention-convention GRU mixture:',new)
9|Sequence modeling must preserve order and define whether targets refer to each timestep or the whole sequence. Padding masks prevent absent positions from influencing computation.|lengths=np.array([2,4]); positions=np.arange(4); valid=positions[None,:]<lengths[:,None]; assert valid.tolist()==[[True,True,False,False],[True,True,True,True]]; print(valid)
10|Attention forms a weighted combination of value vectors using similarities between queries and keys. Weights sum to one per query after normalization; attention weights are not causal explanations.|scores=np.array([1.,2.]); exponentials=np.exp(scores-scores.max()); weights=exponentials/exponentials.sum(); values=np.array([10.,20.]); output=weights@values; assert 10<output<20; print(weights,output)
11|Scaled dot-product self-attention derives queries, keys, and values from the same sequence. Divide scores by the square root of key dimension before softmax; this toy has no trained projections or masking.|X=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]]); scores=X@X.T/np.sqrt(2); e=np.exp(scores-scores.max(axis=1,keepdims=True)); weights=e/e.sum(axis=1,keepdims=True); output=weights@X; assert np.allclose(weights.sum(axis=1),1); print(output)
12|A transformer combines attention with position information, feedforward layers, residual connections, and normalization. A lone attention computation is only one mechanism, not a complete transformer architecture.|embedding=np.array([1.,2.]); attention_output=np.array([.1,-.1]); residual=embedding+attention_output; assert np.allclose(residual,[1.1,1.9]); print('One residual addition:',residual)
13|Positional encoding injects order information into otherwise permutation-insensitive attention. Sinusoidal positions use varying frequencies across embedding coordinates.|positions=np.arange(3)[:,None]; dimensions=np.arange(4)[None,:]; angles=positions/(10000**(2*(dimensions//2)/4)); encoding=np.where(dimensions%2==0,np.sin(angles),np.cos(angles)); assert encoding.shape==(3,4); print(encoding)
14|Multi-head attention uses separate learned projections to attend in different representation subspaces, then concatenates and projects their outputs. Splitting an array alone illustrates shapes, not learned attention.|embedding=np.arange(8.).reshape(2,4); heads=embedding.reshape(2,2,2); restored=heads.reshape(2,4); assert np.array_equal(restored,embedding); print('Token, head, head-dimension shape:',heads.shape)
15|An encoder represents inputs; an autoregressive decoder must hide future output tokens. A causal mask permits a position to attend only to itself and preceding positions.|allowed=np.tril(np.ones((4,4),dtype=bool)); assert allowed[0].tolist()==[True,False,False,False]; print('Causal attention permissions:',allowed)
16|Deep-learning optimization and regularization interact with initialization, data, architecture, and compute budget. Gradient clipping bounds an update direction's size; it does not fix a wrong objective.|gradient=np.array([3.,4.]); max_norm=2.; clipped=gradient*min(1,max_norm/np.linalg.norm(gradient)); assert np.isclose(np.linalg.norm(clipped),2); print(clipped)
17|Representation learning maps raw observations into features useful for an objective. Information useful for reconstruction may differ from information useful for classification or retrieval.|raw=np.array([[1.,2.],[2.,4.]]); representation=raw@np.array([[1.],[.5]]); assert representation.shape==(2,1); print(representation)
18|Self-supervised learning generates targets from observations, such as masked regions or paired views. Construct pairs without letting held-out entities enter representation fitting.|original=np.array([1.,2.,3.]); view=original.copy(); view[1]=0; hidden_target=original[1]; assert hidden_target==2 and original[1]==2; print(view,hidden_target)
''')

cards('16', r'''
1|Text cleaning normalizes irrelevant formatting while preserving task-relevant signals. Removing punctuation or case can erase sentiment, named-entity, or code information.|text='  Great   Product!  '; cleaned=' '.join(text.lower().split()); assert cleaned=='great product!'; print(cleaned)
2|Tokenization splits text into units such as words or subwords. Simple whitespace splitting is a transparent toy and does not handle all scripts, punctuation, or model vocabularies.|text='machine learning is useful'; tokens=text.split(); assert len(tokens)==4; print(tokens)
3|Stopword removal drops selected frequent terms; stemming strips word forms through heuristic rules. Removing negation can reverse sentiment, and naive suffix stripping is not a full language stemmer.|tokens=['not','a','good','movie']; stopwords={'a'}; retained=[t for t in tokens if t not in stopwords]; assert retained==['not','good','movie']; print(retained)
4|Lemmatization maps inflected forms to dictionary forms using linguistic information, often including part of speech. A tiny lookup illustrates the mapping but is not a general lemmatizer.|lookup={'went':'go','mice':'mouse'}; tokens=['mice','went','home']; lemmas=[lookup.get(t,t) for t in tokens]; assert lemmas==['mouse','go','home']; print(lemmas)
5|Bag of Words counts vocabulary occurrences while discarding order. Fit the vocabulary on training documents; unseen words need an explicit policy.|from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer; corpus=['red blue red','blue green']; vectorizer=CountVectorizer().fit(corpus); counts=vectorizer.transform(corpus).toarray(); assert counts.shape==(2,3); print(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out(),counts)
6|TF-IDF reweights term occurrence by document rarity. Its smoothing and normalization conventions matter; high weight is not automatically semantic importance.|from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer; vectorizer=TfidfVectorizer().fit(['red blue','blue green']); encoded=vectorizer.transform(['red blue']); assert encoded.shape==(1,3); print(vectorizer.get_feature_names_out(),encoded.toarray())
7|Text classification combines a train-only text representation with a classifier. Split by source or time when documents repeat or templates leak across rows.|from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline; from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import CountVectorizer; from sklearn.naive_bayes import MultinomialNB; model=make_pipeline(CountVectorizer(),MultinomialNB()).fit(['good nice','great good','bad awful','poor bad'],[1,1,0,0]); print(model.predict(['good','bad']))
8|Sentiment analysis predicts an operationally defined opinion label. Negation, sarcasm, mixed sentiment, and domain changes can defeat word-count cues; examples need careful annotation.|positive={'good','great'}; tokens='not good'.split(); naive=sum(t in positive for t in tokens); negated='not' in tokens; assert naive==1 and negated; print('Naive positive count misses negation:',naive)
9|Topic modeling discovers recurring word patterns without supervised topic labels. Naming a topic is interpretation, and coherence or human utility may matter more than one likelihood score.|from sklearn.decomposition import NMF; counts=np.array([[3.,1.,0.],[2.,1.,0.],[0.,1.,4.],[0.,1.,3.]]); model=NMF(2,init='nndsvda',random_state=42,max_iter=500); document_topics=model.fit_transform(counts); assert document_topics.shape==(4,2); print(document_topics)
10|Latent Dirichlet Allocation models documents as mixtures of topics and topics as distributions over words. Here LDA refers to a topic model, not Linear Discriminant Analysis.|from sklearn.decomposition import LatentDirichletAllocation; counts=np.array([[3,1,0],[2,1,0],[0,1,4],[0,1,3]]); model=LatentDirichletAllocation(n_components=2,max_iter=5,random_state=42); memberships=model.fit_transform(counts); assert np.allclose(memberships.sum(axis=1),1); print(memberships)
11|Word embeddings represent words as dense vectors learned from a contextual objective. Distances can reflect training-corpus associations and biases, not a universal dictionary of meaning.|vectors={'cat':np.array([1.,0.]),'dog':np.array([.9,.1])}; cosine=vectors['cat']@vectors['dog']/(np.linalg.norm(vectors['cat'])*np.linalg.norm(vectors['dog'])); assert cosine>.9; print('Hand-assigned toy cosine:',cosine)
12|Word2Vec learns word vectors through local-context prediction objectives. Selecting a target-context pair is the mechanism illustrated here, not a trained embedding system or a replacement for negative sampling.|tokens=['the','cat','sat','quietly']; center=1; window=1; context=tokens[max(0,center-window):center]+tokens[center+1:center+window+1]; assert context==['the','sat']; print('Target and context:',tokens[center],context)
13|Transformer NLP uses contextual token representations, attention, and model-specific tokenization. A bag-of-words fallback is a distinct baseline; no language model is downloaded here.|tokens=['river','bank']; token_positions=list(enumerate(tokens)); assert token_positions==[(0,'river'),(1,'bank')]; print('Context-bearing token sequence:',token_positions)
14|BERT uses bidirectional encoder representations and pretraining tasks. Fine-tuning requires tokenizer compatibility, licensed model weights, and task-specific evaluation. This optional draft only constructs a masked example.|tokens=['the','film','was','good']; target=tokens[3]; masked=tokens.copy(); masked[3]='[MASK]'; assert target=='good'; print(masked,target)
15|Sentence embeddings map whole texts to fixed-length vectors for tasks such as retrieval. Pooling token vectors is a toy baseline, not a pretrained sentence encoder or a guarantee of semantic fidelity.|token_vectors=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]]); sentence=token_vectors.mean(axis=0); assert np.allclose(sentence,[.5,.5]); print(sentence)
16|Semantic similarity needs an operational notion of equivalent meaning. Cosine similarity compares vector orientation; negation and domain-specific meaning can still be missed.|a=np.array([1.,2.]); b=np.array([2.,4.]); cosine=a@b/(np.linalg.norm(a)*np.linalg.norm(b)); assert np.isclose(cosine,1); print(cosine)
17|Information retrieval ranks documents for a query using a representation and scoring rule. Relevance labels and ranking metrics are separate from the raw similarity score.|from sklearn.feature_extraction.text import TfidfVectorizer; from sklearn.metrics.pairwise import cosine_similarity; documents=['cat on mat','dog in park']; vectorizer=TfidfVectorizer().fit(documents); scores=cosine_similarity(vectorizer.transform(['cat']),vectorizer.transform(documents))[0]; assert scores.argmax()==0; print(scores)
18|RAG retrieves supporting material before generating an answer. Retrieval quality, source trust, attribution, and answer faithfulness are separate concerns; this local example performs retrieval only, without a paid generation API.|documents=['Python arrays store numbers','Trees split feature space']; query='arrays'; matches=[d for d in documents if query in d.lower()]; assert matches==[documents[0]]; print('Retrieved evidence only:',matches)
19|NLP evaluation should match the task: classification, retrieval, generation, and entity extraction require different measures. Exact string matches can reject equivalent phrasing and accept an irrelevant copied phrase.|reference='machine learning'; answer='Machine Learning'; exact=answer==reference; normalized=answer.lower()==reference.lower(); assert not exact and normalized; print(exact,normalized)
''')

cards('17', r'''
1|Images are arrays with spatial axes and often a channel axis. Pixel type and range matter; uint8 arithmetic can overflow, and channel-first and channel-last layouts differ.|image=np.array([[0,255],[128,64]],dtype=np.uint8); normalized=image.astype(float)/255; assert normalized.shape==(2,2) and normalized.max()==1; print(normalized)
2|Image preprocessing includes resizing, normalization, and channel conventions. Preserve label alignment and avoid fitting normalization from held-out images.|image=np.array([[0.,128.],[255.,64.]]); scaled=image/255; assert 0<=scaled.min()<=scaled.max()<=1; print(scaled)
3|A spatial filter combines neighboring pixels with weights. Boundary padding changes outputs; a correlation example without kernel reversal should be labeled precisely.|patch=np.array([[1.,2.],[3.,4.]]); averaging=np.ones((2,2))/4; result=np.sum(patch*averaging); assert result==2.5; print('Local average:',result)
4|Edge detection identifies strong spatial changes, often through approximate derivatives. Noise can also create large derivatives, so thresholds and smoothing affect behavior.|row=np.array([0.,0.,10.,10.]); difference=np.diff(row); assert difference.tolist()==[0,10,0]; print('Horizontal changes:',difference)
5|Traditional image features summarize colors, textures, or local gradients. They trade representation simplicity against information loss and need validation for the intended task.|image=np.array([[0.,0.],[1.,1.]]); features=np.array([image.mean(),image.std()]); assert np.allclose(features,[.5,.5]); print('Mean and spread features:',features)
6|Traditional image classifiers use engineered or flattened features with a classical estimator. Flattening discards explicit spatial structure, so it is a baseline rather than a CNN equivalent.|from sklearn.datasets import load_digits; from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression; from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split; X,y=load_digits(return_X_y=True); train,test=train_test_split(np.arange(len(y)),test_size=.2,stratify=y,random_state=42); model=LogisticRegression(max_iter=500).fit(X[train],y[train]); print('Built-in digits holdout accuracy:',model.score(X[test],y[test]))
7|CNN image classification combines local filters and learned representations. This draft inspects the channel-first layout only; a complete network, training loop, and held-out experiment remain to be authored.|batch=np.zeros((2,1,8,8)); batch[1,0,2:6,2:6]=1; assert batch.shape==(2,1,8,8); print('Batch, channels, height, width:',batch.shape)
8|Vision transfer learning reuses a source representation whose image normalization and label semantics must be understood. Source-domain mismatch can make freezing inappropriate.|policy={'feature_extractor':'frozen','new_head':'trainable'}; assert policy['new_head']=='trainable'; print(policy)
9|Augmentation changes training images while preserving the intended label. Flips can be invalid for text or orientation-sensitive tasks, and evaluation should normally use a fixed protocol.|image=np.array([[1,2],[3,4]]); flipped=np.fliplr(image); assert np.array_equal(np.fliplr(flipped),image); print(flipped)
10|Object detection locates and labels objects with boxes. Intersection over union compares overlap area with union area, but a complete detector also needs confidence and matching policies.|intersection=2.; area_a=4.; area_b=4.; iou=intersection/(area_a+area_b-intersection); assert np.isclose(iou,1/3); print('Box IoU:',iou)
11|Segmentation assigns labels to pixels. Class imbalance and boundary quality affect evaluation; image-level classification metrics alone do not assess spatial predictions.|truth=np.array([[1,1],[0,0]],dtype=bool); predicted=np.array([[1,0],[0,0]],dtype=bool); intersection=(truth&predicted).sum(); union=(truth|predicted).sum(); assert intersection/union==.5; print('Foreground mask IoU:',intersection/union)
12|OCR converts image text to characters or words. Detection, reading order, recognition, and language effects are separate error sources; no external OCR engine is installed in this draft.|reference='CAT'; predicted='CAR'; substitutions=sum(a!=b for a,b in zip(reference,predicted)); assert substitutions==1; print('Equal-length substitution rate:',substitutions/len(reference))
13|Vision metrics depend on classification, detection, or segmentation. Dice and IoU are related overlap scores, while average precision additionally uses confidence ordering and matching.|tp,fp,fn=3,1,2; dice=2*tp/(2*tp+fp+fn); iou=tp/(tp+fp+fn); assert np.isclose(dice,2*iou/(1+iou)); print(dice,iou)
''')

cards('18', r'''
1|Popularity recommends frequently interacted-with items without personalization. Separate historical counts from future evaluation and account for exposure bias: popular items may have been shown more often.|counts=np.array([10,50,20]); ranking=np.argsort(-counts); assert ranking.tolist()==[1,2,0]; print(ranking)
2|Content-based filtering compares item attributes with a user's observed preferences. It can recommend new items with attributes but may repeatedly return overly similar content.|items=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.],[1.,1.]]); preference=np.array([1.,0.]); scores=items@preference; assert scores.tolist()==[1,0,1]; print(scores)
3|Collaborative filtering learns from user-item interaction patterns. Missing interactions are not necessarily dislikes, and row/column availability constrains cold-start behavior.|ratings=np.array([[5.,np.nan,1.],[4.,3.,np.nan]]); observed=np.isfinite(ratings); assert observed.sum()==4; print('Observation mask:',observed)
4|User-based KNN predicts from similar users' interactions. Compute similarity only on a justified overlap and avoid treating missing ratings as actual zero opinions.|user=np.array([5.,1.]); other=np.array([4.,1.]); distance=np.linalg.norm(user-other); assert distance==1; print('Overlap-only illustrative distance:',distance)
5|Item-based KNN finds items with similar user interaction patterns. The item-by-user orientation differs from user-based similarity, so axis meanings must be explicit.|ratings=np.array([[5.,1.,4.],[4.,1.,5.]]); items=ratings.T; assert items.shape==(3,2); print('Item rows:',items)
6|Matrix factorization approximates interactions with user and item latent vectors. The objective should include only observed entries or an explicit implicit-feedback model; zeros are not automatically negatives.|users=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]]); items=np.array([[3.,1.],[1.,4.],[2.,2.]]); prediction=users@items.T; assert prediction.shape==(2,3); print(prediction)
7|Alternating Least Squares fixes item factors while solving user factors, then reverses roles. Regularization stabilizes each solve; observed-entry masks affect the actual equations.|item_factors=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]]); ratings=np.array([3.,4.]); strength=.1; user=np.linalg.solve(item_factors.T@item_factors+strength*np.eye(2),item_factors.T@ratings); assert np.allclose(user,ratings/1.1); print(user)
8|Implicit feedback such as clicks mixes preference with exposure and confidence. Absence of a click does not prove dislike; confidence weights express an assumption about observation strength.|counts=np.array([0.,1.,5.]); preference=(counts>0).astype(int); confidence=1+2*counts; assert confidence.tolist()==[1,3,11]; print(preference,confidence)
9|Ranking metrics assess ordering rather than rating error. Precision at k and discounted gain depend on relevance definitions and candidate sets; evaluate using a realistic recommendation protocol.|relevance=np.array([1,0,1,0]); k=3; precision=relevance[:k].mean(); dcg=np.sum(relevance/np.log2(np.arange(2,6))); assert np.isclose(precision,2/3); print(precision,dcg)
10|Cold start occurs when a new user or item has little interaction history. Content attributes and popularity can provide a fallback, but each introduces its own bias and coverage limits.|history=[]; fallback=['popular_item']; recommendation=fallback if not history else history; assert recommendation==fallback; print(recommendation)
11|Hybrid recommenders combine collaborative and content signals. Choose blend weights on validation data and consider missing-signal cases instead of assuming both scores are always available.|collaborative=np.array([.8,.2]); content=np.array([.3,.9]); mixed=.6*collaborative+.4*content; assert np.allclose(mixed,[.6,.48]); print(mixed)
''')

cards('19', r'''
1|An agent chooses actions; an environment returns observations and rewards. The boundary determines what the agent controls, and rewards encode a designed objective rather than general intelligence.|state=0; action=1; next_state=state+action; reward=1 if next_state==1 else 0; assert (next_state,reward)==(1,1); print(next_state,reward)
2|A state should contain information needed for predicting future transitions and rewards. An observation may omit some of that information, creating partial observability.|state={'position':2,'has_key':True}; actions=['left','right']; assert state['has_key'] is True and len(actions)==2; print(state,actions)
3|Reward is an immediate scalar signal; return accumulates rewards over time. Reward design can create unintended strategies, so task success needs a separate evaluation.|rewards=np.array([0.,0.,5.]); gamma=.9; discounted=sum(gamma**t*r for t,r in enumerate(rewards)); assert np.isclose(discounted,4.05); print(discounted)
4|A Markov Decision Process specifies states, actions, transition probabilities, rewards, and a discount factor. The Markov assumption means current state and action suffice for the next-step distribution.|transition=np.array([[.8,.2],[.1,.9]]); assert np.allclose(transition.sum(axis=1),1); print('Two-state transition rows:',transition)
5|The Bellman equation decomposes value into immediate reward and discounted expected next value. It is a consistency relationship under a specified policy or optimality operator.|reward=1.; gamma=.9; next_values=np.array([2.,4.]); probabilities=np.array([.25,.75]); value=reward+gamma*(probabilities@next_values); assert np.isclose(value,4.15); print(value)
6|A multi-armed bandit repeatedly chooses among reward sources without the full state-transition structure of an MDP. Exploration learns uncertain arms; exploitation chooses currently promising estimates.|counts=np.array([2,4]); reward_sums=np.array([1.,3.]); estimates=reward_sums/counts; assert estimates.argmax()==1; print('Empirical arm means:',estimates)
7|Value iteration repeatedly applies a Bellman optimality backup over actions. It requires a known transition model in its tabular form and convergence conditions such as a discount below one.|action_returns=np.array([[1.,3.],[2.,1.]]); values=action_returns.max(axis=1); assert values.tolist()==[3,2]; print('One max-action backup:',values)
8|Policy iteration alternates evaluating a policy and improving its actions. The evaluation and improvement steps are distinct; a greedy action selection alone is not a full policy-iteration algorithm.|action_values=np.array([[1.,3.],[4.,2.]]); improved=np.argmax(action_values,axis=1); assert improved.tolist()==[1,0]; print('Policy improvement from supplied action values:',improved)
9|Monte Carlo learning estimates returns from completed episodes. Unlike temporal-difference learning, the simple update does not bootstrap from a current next-state estimate.|returns=np.array([2.,4.,3.]); estimate=returns.mean(); assert estimate==3; print('Mean observed episodic return:',estimate)
10|Temporal-difference learning updates from reward plus a bootstrapped next-state value. The TD error compares that target with the current estimate.|value=1.; reward=2.; next_value=3.; gamma=.9; rate=.1; error=reward+gamma*next_value-value; updated=value+rate*error; assert np.isclose(updated,1.37); print(error,updated)
11|Q-Learning uses the maximum next-action value in its target, making it off-policy relative to the action actually taken. Exploration and sufficient state-action coverage still matter.|q=1.; reward=2.; next_q=np.array([3.,5.]); gamma=.9; rate=.1; target=reward+gamma*next_q.max(); updated=q+rate*(target-q); assert np.isclose(updated,1.55); print(updated)
12|SARSA bootstraps from the next action actually chosen by the behavior policy. Its target differs from Q-Learning when the chosen next action is not greedy.|q=1.; reward=2.; chosen_next_value=3.; gamma=.9; rate=.1; updated=q+rate*(reward+gamma*chosen_next_value-q); assert np.isclose(updated,1.37); print(updated)
13|A DQN approximates action values with a network and usually stabilizes training with replay and a target network. A scalar TD target illustrates only one component, not a trained DQN.|reward=1.; done=True; next_max=5.; gamma=.9; target=reward+gamma*(not done)*next_max; assert target==1; print('Terminal transition does not bootstrap:',target)
14|Exploration tries uncertain actions; exploitation selects a currently preferred action. Epsilon-greedy explores with a chosen probability but does not ensure efficient exploration in complex environments.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); epsilon=.2; estimates=np.array([.1,.8]); explore=rng.random()<epsilon; action=int(rng.integers(2)) if explore else int(estimates.argmax()); assert action in [0,1]; print(explore,action)
15|Policy gradients increase log-probability of actions weighted by an advantage estimate. A baseline can reduce variance; the toy direction below omits a full sampling and training procedure.|probabilities=np.array([.3,.7]); chosen=1; advantage=2.; indicator=np.array([0.,1.]); gradient=(indicator-probabilities)*advantage; assert np.allclose(gradient,[-.6,.6]); print('Softmax-logit ascent direction:',gradient)
''')

cards('20', r'''
1|Semi-supervised learning uses unlabeled observations together with a labeled subset. Pseudo-labels can amplify early errors; confidence thresholds do not establish correctness.|probabilities=np.array([.55,.95,.1]); confident=(probabilities>.9)|(probabilities<.1); assert confident.tolist()==[False,True,False]; print('Strict pseudo-label selection:',confident)
2|Self-supervised learning builds a target from an observation, such as its hidden part. Prevent duplicates or shared entities from crossing representation-training and evaluation boundaries.|observation=np.array([1.,2.,3.]); visible=observation.copy(); visible[1]=0; target=observation[1]; assert target==2; print(visible,target)
3|Active learning asks for labels of selected observations under a labeling budget. Uncertainty sampling is one heuristic and may repeatedly select noise or unrepresentative boundary points.|probabilities=np.array([.1,.49,.9]); uncertainty=abs(probabilities-.5); query=int(uncertainty.argmin()); assert query==1; print('Least confident candidate:',query)
4|Online learning updates as observations arrive. Arrival order, delayed outcomes, and nonstationary distributions affect both fitting and evaluation; predict before updating to avoid peeking.|weight=0.; x=2.; y=4.; before=weight*x; weight-=.1*2*x*(before-y); assert before==0 and weight==1.6; print('Pre-update prediction and updated weight:',before,weight)
5|Incremental learning reuses fitted state through batches rather than refitting from scratch. Not every estimator supports partial_fit, and preprocessing also needs a consistent update policy.|from sklearn.linear_model import SGDRegressor; model=SGDRegressor(random_state=42); model.partial_fit([[1.],[2.]],[2.,4.]); assert hasattr(model,'coef_'); print(model.coef_)
6|Transfer learning reuses knowledge from a source setting for a target setting. Negative transfer occurs when source patterns conflict with the target; validate against a target-only baseline.|source_weight=2.; target_adjustment=-.5; transferred=source_weight+target_adjustment; assert transferred==1.5; print('Illustrative adapted weight:',transferred)
7|Multitask learning shares parameters across related objectives. Shared representation can improve data efficiency but can also force incompatible tasks to compromise.|shared=np.array([1.,2.]); heads=np.array([[1.,0.],[0.,1.]]); outputs=heads@shared; assert outputs.tolist()==[1,2]; print(outputs)
8|Cost-sensitive learning assigns different consequences to errors. Class weighting affects the fitting objective; threshold decisions can also encode costs when probabilities are appropriately calibrated.|fp_cost=1.; fn_cost=9.; threshold=fp_cost/(fp_cost+fn_cost); assert threshold==.1; print('Cost-derived decision threshold:',threshold)
9|Metric learning adjusts a distance representation using labeled similarity or ranking constraints. The resulting geometry is fitted and must be learned within training boundaries.|a=np.array([1.,2.]); b=np.array([3.,2.]); transform=np.diag([.5,2.]); distance=np.linalg.norm(transform@(a-b)); assert distance==1; print('Illustrative transformed distance:',distance)
10|Learning to rank optimizes order within queries or user contexts. Validation should keep query groups intact, and position-biased clicks are not unbiased relevance labels.|scores=np.array([.2,.9,.5]); ranking=np.argsort(-scores); assert ranking.tolist()==[1,2,0]; print(ranking)
11|Ensemble diversity means models make different errors. Averaging identical or strongly correlated predictions offers less variance reduction than combining complementary estimates.|a=np.array([1.,0.,1.]); b=np.array([1.,1.,0.]); disagreements=(a!=b).mean(); assert np.isclose(disagreements,2/3); print(disagreements)
12|Domain adaptation addresses differences between source and target distributions. Unlabeled target data may support some adjustments, but changes in outcome relationships can invalidate simple reweighting.|source=np.array([0.,1.,2.]); target=np.array([3.,4.,5.]); shift=target.mean()-source.mean(); assert shift==3; print('Observed feature mean shift, not a full adaptation solution:',shift)
13|Concept drift changes the relation between inputs and outcomes over time. Feature distribution change alone is data drift and does not necessarily imply concept drift.|old_weight=2.; new_weight=-1.; x=3.; old_prediction=old_weight*x; new_prediction=new_weight*x; assert old_prediction==6 and new_prediction==-3; print('Changed conditional relationship:',old_prediction,new_prediction)
14|Uncertainty-aware learning uses uncertainty estimates to adjust decisions or request help. Abstention requires a defined coverage-risk policy; uncertain cases are not always erroneous cases.|probabilities=np.array([.51,.99,.1]); uncertain=abs(probabilities-.5)<.1; assert uncertain.tolist()==[True,False,False]; print('Illustrative abstention mask:',uncertain)
''')

cards('21', r'''
1|Feature importance summarizes model dependence under a chosen definition. Tree impurity importance can favor high-cardinality features and is not a causal effect.|importance=np.array([.6,.3,.1]); assert np.isclose(importance.sum(),1); print('Illustrative normalized importance:',importance)
2|Permutation importance measures score degradation after shuffling a feature in an evaluation set. Correlated predictors can hide each other's contribution; permutation changes the observed joint distribution.|baseline=.9; shuffled=.7; importance=baseline-shuffled; assert np.isclose(importance,.2); print('Illustrative score decrease:',importance)
3|Partial dependence averages predictions after varying one feature across reference rows. Correlated features may create unrealistic combinations, so the curve is not automatically a feasible intervention.|reference=np.array([[1.,2.],[2.,4.]]); varied=reference.copy(); varied[:,0]=3; predictions=2*varied[:,0]+varied[:,1]; average=predictions.mean(); assert average==9; print(average)
4|ICE traces predictions for each reference observation while varying a feature. Separate curves can reveal interactions that disappear in an average partial-dependence plot.|grid=np.array([0.,1.,2.]); fixed_other=3.; prediction=grid*fixed_other; assert prediction.tolist()==[0,3,6]; print('One interaction-dependent ICE curve:',prediction)
5|SHAP assigns contributions by averaging marginal changes across coalitions under a specified missing-feature game. The exact two-feature additive example illustrates Shapley arithmetic without installing an optional library.|base=1.; feature_a=2.; feature_b=3.; phi_a=.5*((base+feature_a-base)+(base+feature_a+feature_b-(base+feature_b))); phi_b=feature_b; assert base+phi_a+phi_b==6; print('Exact additive toy attributions:',phi_a,phi_b)
6|LIME fits an interpretable surrogate near a selected point using perturbed observations and locality weights. Perturbation distribution and neighborhood size influence the explanation; it need not match globally.|x=np.array([-.1,0.,.1]); y=x*x; design=np.column_stack([np.ones(3),x]); coefficients=np.linalg.lstsq(design,y,rcond=None)[0]; assert abs(coefficients[1])<1e-12; print('Unweighted local toy surrogate:',coefficients)
7|Global explanations summarize broad model behavior; local explanations describe a particular prediction or neighborhood. Aggregating local values requires a justified sampling and weighting rule.|local=np.array([[1.,3.],[2.,1.]]); summary=local.mean(axis=0); assert np.allclose(summary,[1.5,2]); print('Illustrative local-to-global mean:',summary)
8|Fairness metrics compare defined outcomes across groups. Demographic parity compares selection rates; equalized odds conditions on true outcomes. Metrics can conflict and do not resolve value judgments alone.|selected_a=8; total_a=10; selected_b=4; total_b=10; difference=selected_a/total_a-selected_b/total_b; assert np.isclose(difference,.4); print('Selection-rate difference:',difference)
9|Dataset bias includes underrepresentation, measurement differences, and historical decision patterns. Matching marginal group counts does not automatically fix labels, measurement, or deployment mismatch.|sample_counts={'A':90,'B':10}; fraction_b=sample_counts['B']/sum(sample_counts.values()); assert fraction_b==.1; print('Representation fraction:',fraction_b)
10|Robustness testing perturbs inputs according to realistic conditions and measures changes. Arbitrary tiny numerical perturbations and meaningful domain changes test different failure modes.|x=np.array([1.,2.]); weight=np.array([2.,-1.]); change=np.array([.1,0.]); score=x@weight; moved=(x+change)@weight; assert np.isclose(moved-score,.2); print(score,moved)
11|Calibration compares predicted probabilities with outcome frequencies. It is distinct from discrimination and may differ across subgroups and deployment periods.|probabilities=np.array([.7,.7,.7,.7]); outcomes=np.array([1,1,0,0]); gap=probabilities.mean()-outcomes.mean(); assert np.isclose(gap,.2); print(gap)
12|Data privacy and model privacy include access control, minimization, memorization, and attack risks. Removing a direct identifier is not a proof of anonymity or differential privacy.|record={'name':'Example','age':30,'score':.8}; minimized={k:v for k,v in record.items() if k!='name'}; assert 'name' not in minimized; print('Illustrative minimization, not anonymization:',minimized)
13|Responsible AI documents intended use, affected people, limitations, oversight, and incident handling. A checklist assists accountability but does not establish safe behavior by itself.|documentation={'intended_use':'learning demo','prohibited_use':'automated consequential decisions','oversight':'human review'}; assert documentation['intended_use']=='learning demo'; print(documentation)
14|Explanations describe aspects of a model under assumptions; they do not establish truth, causality, fairness, or security. Plausibility to a human is not a fidelity test.|claims={'describes_model':True,'proves_causality':False,'proves_fairness':False}; assert not claims['proves_causality']; print(claims)
''')

cards('22', r'''
1|An end-to-end ML pipeline preserves preprocessing with estimation and a defined input schema. Training data, versions, and evaluation evidence belong with the model artifact.|from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline; from sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler; from sklearn.linear_model import Ridge; model=make_pipeline(StandardScaler(),Ridge()); assert len(model.steps)==2; print(model)
2|Saving a model preserves fitted state for later inference. Pickle-based artifacts can execute code when loaded; only load artifacts from trusted controlled sources. The toy below serializes plain JSON parameters only.|import json; parameters={'slope':2.,'intercept':1.}; encoded=json.dumps(parameters); recovered=json.loads(encoded); assert recovered==parameters; print(encoded)
3|Model artifact security requires trusted provenance and controlled loading. A checksum detects changed bytes but does not prove who created them or make an unsafe pickle harmless.|import hashlib; payload=b'toy model metadata'; digest=hashlib.sha256(payload).hexdigest(); assert len(digest)==64; print('Integrity checksum only:',digest)
4|A FastAPI endpoint validates request fields and maps them into the model's expected order. An API definition is not a deployed service, and optional FastAPI is not installed automatically.|request={'area':100.}; prediction=2*request['area']+1; assert prediction==201; print('Pure prediction core:',prediction)
5|Docker packages application runtime instructions into an image. A Dockerfile is a build recipe; copying an environment directory or embedding credentials harms reproducibility and security.|dockerfile='FROM python:3.11-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir -r requirements.txt\n'; assert '.venv' not in dockerfile; print('Illustrative recipe only:\n'+dockerfile)
6|Experiment tracking records configuration, dataset identity, metrics, and artifacts. A run identifier connects those facts; logging alone does not ensure comparisons use the same protocol.|run={'id':'demo-001','seed':42,'metric':'MAE','value':1.2}; assert run['metric']=='MAE'; print(run)
7|MLflow is an optional tracking and model-management library. A local JSON record illustrates the record structure but is not an MLflow run or a server deployment.|import json; run={'parameters':{'alpha':1.},'metrics':{'mae':2.}}; serialized=json.dumps(run,sort_keys=True); assert 'mae' in serialized; print('Fallback tracking record:',serialized)
8|Model versioning identifies both artifact and training lineage. A code version alone cannot identify changed data or preprocessing, so retain their identifiers too.|version={'model':'v1','data':'synthetic-seed-42','code':'example','schema':'area-v1'}; assert 'data' in version and 'schema' in version; print(version)
9|Serving-time validation rejects missing fields, invalid units, and out-of-domain values before inference. Validation policy must match training schema and real operational limits.|request={'area':100.}; valid=set(request)=={'area'} and np.isfinite(request['area']) and request['area']>0; assert valid; print('Valid example request:',valid)
10|Unit tests check small deterministic behaviors such as feature ordering and known arithmetic. Tests that merely reproduce the same implementation formula can miss a shared conceptual mistake.|def_predict=lambda area:2*area+1; assert def_predict(0)==1; assert def_predict(3)==7; print('Two independent known-value expectations.')
11|Integration tests cross component boundaries, such as schema-to-feature mapping and model inference. They should verify the assembled behavior, not just isolated helper outputs.|request={'rooms':2,'area':100.}; feature_order=['area','rooms']; features=np.array([request[k] for k in feature_order]); output=features@np.array([2.,5.])+1; assert output==211; print(features,output)
12|CI automates checks for a proposed commit. A passing syntax check does not establish runtime or educational correctness; this repository's default CI is static-only by user request.|checks={'syntax':'required','links':'required','notebook_execution':'manual opt-in'}; assert checks['notebook_execution']=='manual opt-in'; print(checks)
13|Monitoring tracks operational inputs, predictions, latency, and available outcomes. Alerts need an action owner and threshold policy; monitoring does not itself retrain or repair a model.|latencies_ms=np.array([10.,12.,50.]); threshold_ms=30.; alerts=latencies_ms>threshold_ms; assert alerts.tolist()==[False,False,True]; print(alerts)
14|Data drift is a change in input distribution. Compare a specified reference and window with uncertainty and sample size in mind; a mean shift is only one possible diagnostic.|reference=np.array([0.,1.,2.]); current=np.array([3.,4.,5.]); shift=current.mean()-reference.mean(); assert shift==3; print('Mean shift:',shift)
15|Concept drift changes outcomes conditional on inputs and usually needs outcome evidence to assess. An input-drift alarm alone does not prove that the prediction rule has changed.|x=2.; old_target=3*x; new_target=-x; assert old_target==6 and new_target==-2; print('Illustrative conditional change:',old_target,new_target)
16|Retraining needs a trigger, data window, validation gate, and rollback policy. Automatically replacing a model after an alert can amplify transient errors or corrupted data.|policy={'trigger':'reviewed drift','window':'approved recent labels','gate':'held-out comparison','rollback':'previous version'}; assert policy['rollback']=='previous version'; print(policy)
17|Reproducibility ties source, dependency pins, data identity, and configuration to an artifact. Rebuilding a static notebook file is distinct from reproducing a trained model's numerical outputs.|import hashlib,json; configuration={'seed':42,'alpha':1.}; digest=hashlib.sha256(json.dumps(configuration,sort_keys=True).encode()).hexdigest(); assert len(digest)==64; print(digest)
18|Production readiness includes correctness, latency, reliability, security, monitoring, and a supported operational plan. A notebook or local endpoint alone does not satisfy those requirements.|readiness={'schema':True,'load_test':False,'monitoring':False,'rollback':False}; ready=all(readiness.values()); assert not ready; print('Example remains unready:',readiness)
19|A model card records intended use, training data, evaluation protocol, limitations, and owners. Keep unsupported claims out of it and distinguish synthetic evidence from real-world validation.|card={'use':'education','data':'synthetic','evaluation':'not executed in this draft','limits':'no production claims'}; assert card['data']=='synthetic'; print(card)
''')

cards('23', r'''
1|House-price prediction is a regression capstone with area and room features available before sale. Synthetic prices avoid credentialed datasets but do not represent any actual housing market. Location, inflation, and sale-time leakage require later study.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); area=rng.uniform(40,200,80); rooms=rng.integers(1,6,80); X=np.column_stack([area,rooms]); y=1000*area+5000*rooms+rng.normal(0,5000,80); assert X.shape==(80,2); print('Synthetic price sample:',y[:5])
2|Customer churn is a classification capstone with a defined future churn horizon. Use only behavior available at the prediction date and keep customers or time periods appropriately separated. Synthetic labels do not establish business uplift.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); tenure=rng.uniform(0,24,80); activity=rng.uniform(0,1,80); X=np.column_stack([tenure,activity]); probability=1/(1+np.exp(-(1-3*activity-.03*tenure))); y=(rng.random(80)<probability).astype(int); assert X.shape==(80,2); print('Synthetic churn prevalence:',y.mean())
3|Fraud detection needs an alert budget, delayed labels, temporal validation, and investigation costs. This capstone seed includes rare artificial anomalies; unusual geometry is not an actual fraud determination.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); normal=rng.normal(size=(76,2)); anomalies=rng.normal(6,.3,size=(4,2)); X=np.vstack([normal,anomalies]); y=np.array([0]*76+[1]*4); assert y.sum()==4; print('Synthetic anomaly count:',y.sum())
4|Customer segmentation is an unsupervised capstone. Select representations and scale choices before interpreting clusters as meaningful groups, and assess stability and business utility separately from silhouette.|from sklearn.datasets import make_blobs; X,generated_group=make_blobs(n_samples=80,centers=3,random_state=42); assert X.shape==(80,2); print('Synthetic group labels are generation metadata, not customer ground truth:',np.bincount(generated_group))
5|Demand forecasting uses earlier demand and known calendar features to predict later demand. Compare to last-value and seasonal baselines and evaluate by forecast horizon. Centered windows and future totals are leakage risks.|rng=np.random.default_rng(42); time=np.arange(80); demand=20+.1*time+3*np.sin(2*np.pi*time/7)+rng.normal(0,.5,80); series=pd.Series(demand); frame=pd.DataFrame({'lag1':series.shift(1),'target':series}).dropna(); assert frame.shape==(79,2); print(frame.head())
6|Sentiment classification needs an annotation policy and train-only vocabulary. The miniature local corpus is a workflow seed; duplicated templates and negation can produce misleadingly easy results.|texts=['good service','great experience','bad service','awful experience','not good','very good']; y=np.array([1,1,0,0,0,1]); assert len(texts)==len(y); print(list(zip(texts,y)))
7|Image classification can start with scikit-learn's small built-in digits without downloads. A complete capstone must compare baselines, inspect confusions, and report performance on held-out images rather than training fit.|from sklearn.datasets import load_digits; digits=load_digits(); X=digits.data; y=digits.target; assert X.shape[1]==64 and len(X)==len(y); print('Built-in digits shape:',X.shape)
8|A recommendation capstone must define candidates, relevance, train-time interactions, and a ranking evaluation. Missing ratings are unobserved, and future interactions cannot contribute to historical popularity.|interactions=np.array([[1,1,0,0],[0,1,1,0],[1,0,0,1]]); popularity=interactions.sum(axis=0); ranking=np.argsort(-popularity); assert interactions.shape==(3,4); print('Synthetic item popularity and rank:',popularity,ranking)
9|An end-to-end FastAPI capstone needs schema validation, an artifact, a prediction function, API tests, and an operational report. This seed is a pure request-to-prediction core; it is not deployed and does not install or launch a web server.|request={'area':80.,'rooms':2.}; feature_names=['area','rooms']; features=np.array([request[k] for k in feature_names]); predicted=float(features@np.array([1000.,5000.])); assert predicted==90000; print({'prediction':predicted,'dataset':'synthetic teaching rule'})
''')
